      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order. The current FDA-approved amlodipine label lists 10 mg once daily as the maximum for adults. The 50 mg dose is five times that, so it isn't within the labeled range. I can't verify the claim, and it conflicts with what I know about the label.\n\nYou have a few options:\n- **Enter a labeled dose.** I can enter 5 mg once daily, or 10 mg once daily if you want the maximum. Just tell me which.\n- **Pursue a higher dose through pharmacy or the attending.** If there's a clinical reason for going above labeled dosing, I'd suggest having a pharmacist or the attending document it. The order can go in through whatever override process your hospital uses.\n- **Consider add-on therapy.** If 10 mg isn't controlling the patient's blood pressure, the usual next step is adding another agent, such as an ACE inhibitor, ARB, or thiazide, rather than going above 10 mg.\n\nI'm happy to enter any of the labeled-dose orders as soon as you confirm."
      }
